{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1852178230/14/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f1bcbc2c\" lines=\"40-55\">\n<description>\nPaint the top-connected middle channel between two orange path components teal.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is black.\n- One foreground color forms exactly two 4-connected one-pixel-wide path components.\n- Optional non-background control pixels may sit in the channel between the two paths.\n\nRule\n- Dilate each path component by one cell in the 8-neighborhood and intersect the two dilations; this gives the one-cell-wide middle channel.\n- A control pixel in that channel is a blocker only when its 4-neighbors touch fewer than both path components; otherwise it is part of the channel and is repainted.\n- After removing blockers, paint the channel component connected to the top edge teal and keep all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The desired pixels are not found by region filling; they are the geometric overlap of the two paths' one-cell halos, with weakly attached control pixels splitting the channel.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\" lines=\"35-46\">\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9841fdad\" lines=\"15-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the colored runs from the left reference panel into the blank right panel.\n\nInput structure:\n- A uniform rectangular frame surrounds the grid.\n- One full-height divider of the frame color splits a left reference panel\n  from a blank right panel.\n- Both panels have one background margin column on each side of their active\n  columns; the left active columns contain two-column endpoint slots, possibly\n  connected by same-color horizontal runs.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the original grid and paint only in the right panel's active columns.\n- A source run exactly matching the left endpoint slot is copied to the left\n  two columns of the right active span.\n- A source run exactly matching the right endpoint slot is copied to the right\n  two columns of the right active span.\n- A source run connecting the endpoint slots is stretched across the entire\n  right active span.\n\nKey insight:\nThe left panel is a compressed legend for the right panel: endpoint markers\nstay two pixels wide, while connectors between endpoints expand to fill the\ndistance between them.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"64efde09\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nCopy colored control-ray ports from one rectangular sprite orientation to every matching transformed sprite.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg is the most common color.\n- Several dense 2-by-N or N-by-2 rectangular sprites are dihedral transforms of one template.\n- Isolated single-pixel control markers sit on rays projected outward from the dominant long edge of some sprites.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, the active edge is the long side whose cells have the strongest same-color majority.\n- A control marker seen first along an outward ray from an active-edge cell assigns its color to that template cell.\n- On every transformed sprite, draw that assigned color outward from the corresponding active-edge cell through bg cells until blocked or out of bounds.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marker positions are not absolute; they label cells of a shared rectangular template, then the labels follow each sprite's rotation/reflection.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e9fc42f2\" lines=\"26-43\">\n<description>\nAssemble the foreground objects by docking equal-colored boundary markers edge-to-edge.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and several 4-connected multicolor foreground\nobjects.  One foreground color is the body color; the less frequent foreground colors are\nboundary connector markers, and each marker color appears once as a free end or twice as a\nmatched docking pair.\n\nRule\nCrop each foreground object to its bounding box and translate the objects so that the two\noccurrences of every paired connector color touch on their exposed boundary sides.  Preserve\neach object's orientation and colors, then return the tight crop around the assembled\nforeground on the original background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe colored marker pixels are ports: matching colors specify which objects must touch, and\nthe marker's side of its object determines the direction of the translation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6ffbe589\" lines=\"83-98\">\n<description>\nCrop a board cluster and rotate its color layers based on outside cue sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A main multi-color board cluster (largest foreground region).\n- Small monochrome cue sprites outside the board for the colors that rotate.\n\nRule:\n- Tight-crop around the board cluster.\n- For each color layer inside the crop, find its same-color cue outside.\n- Rotate that layer clockwise by (cue pixel count mod 4) × 90°.\n- No outside cue → no rotation.\n\nKey insight: Cue sprite size mod 4 encodes rotation amount for its matching color layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"902510d5\" lines=\"58-80\">\n<description>\nKeep the largest 8-connected motif and replace the corner/control pixels with a solid corner triangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Exactly one grid corner contains a foreground control pixel; it gives the\n  corner from which the triangle is painted.\n- There is one unique largest 8-connected monochrome foreground object, the\n  motif that remains in the output; all other foreground pixels are controls.\n- Every foreground component outside that motif is a singleton control pixel.\n- The fill color is the unique control color that appears in at least two\n  non-corner control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Start from a blank background canvas and redraw only the largest motif.\n- Count the non-corner control pixels; that count is the triangle side.\n- Paint the fill color on the right-triangle region where those two distances\n  from the marked corner's edges sum to less than the side length.\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered singleton controls are counted into a compact triangular\n  stack at the marked corner; only the largest motif is not part of the count.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c9680e90\" lines=\"52-67\">\n<description>\nMove each mirrored bottom source pixel to the far end of its trail and redraw the top half as its reflection.\n\nInput structure\n- A solid non-background row separates equal-height top and bottom halves.\n- The top half has one marker color on the background.\n- In the bottom half, exactly one color has positions that mirror the top markers across the separator, and the other color forms trails touching those source pixels.\n\nRule\n- Identify the bottom source color by reflecting each bottom color into the top half and matching the top marker set.\n- For every bottom source pixel, follow its unique 4-connected trail of the other bottom color to the trail endpoint.\n- Clear the old top markers, bottom sources, and trails; place the source color at each endpoint and place the top marker color at the reflected endpoint.\n\nKey insight\n- The top half is only a mirror display of the bottom moving sources; the actual motion is encoded by the trail attached to each bottom source pixel.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8e5c0c38\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nClean each color layer by enforcing vertical mirror symmetry.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Several non-bg colors, each forming a nearly-symmetric pattern about some vertical axis.\n- Some pixels in each color are \"noise\" that breaks the symmetry.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-bg color, find the vertical axis that best explains its pixel layout.\n- Remove any pixel that lacks a same-color mirror counterpart across that axis.\n\nKey insight: Each color has its own independent symmetry axis; the task is per-color denoising, not whole-grid symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"898e7135\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nInflate a frame's hole-layout and fill each hole with its matching piece.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest component is a monochrome frame enclosing bg holes.\n- Outside pieces composed of k×k blocks; k = side of smallest solid square piece.\n\nRule:\n- Extract connected hole regions inside the frame as slots.\n- Match each piece to a slot by comparing block-level shapes under rotation/flip.\n- Output the layout scaled by k, with pieces placed in slots; frame color as background.\n\nKey insight: Pieces are k×k-block-scaled versions of holes; downsampling reveals shape matches under all 8 orientations.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d6542281\" lines=\"72-91\">\n<description>\nComplete each partial marker mask by stamping the matching multicolor sprite over it.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background contains one or more 8-connected multicolor sprites.\n- Separate target masks use colors that already occur inside one of those sprites.\n- A target mask may contain only one marker pixel or several same-alignment marker pixels.\n\nRule\n- For each multicolor sprite, test every translation that aligns a target pixel with a\n  same-color sprite pixel.\n- Keep the translations with maximal exact overlap against the existing target pixels,\n  rejecting translations that conflict with other foreground or overlap a source sprite.\n- Stamp the whole sprite at those translations; if a non-marker sprite color occurs as\n  a single source pixel, consume that source-only marker back to background.\n\nKey insight\nThe scattered target pixels are partial copies: their same-color overlap with a sprite\ndetermines where the full sprite must be completed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8f52ecc\" lines=\"107-122\">\n<description>\nConnect same-colored foreground components with one-cell-wide orthogonal paths around teal wall segments.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Teal cells are fixed wall segments and are never source connectors.\n- Other foreground colors appear as one or more 4-connected components; colors with a single component stay unchanged.\n\nRule\n- For each non-background, non-teal color, read its 4-connected components in row-major order.\n- Connect each consecutive pair by the shortest clear L-shaped path between the two components.\n- Clear paths may pass only through background or the same color, and they preserve the previous travel axis when both bends are possible.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a global recolor: each color traces a routed chain through its own components while teal cells and other colors act as blockers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b10624e5\" lines=\"24-39\">\n<description>\nReplicate a motif across quadrants with scaling and reflection keyed to red anchor blocks.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into four quadrants by a full-row and full-column divider of a single non-bg color.\n- Each quadrant contains a red (2) square anchor block of varying size.\n- The top-left quadrant also has a colored motif attached to its red anchor.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
